# CLAUDE.md

This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.

## Project

{{ONE_LINE_PRODUCT_DESCRIPTION}}. Built with the `physical-product` skill:
parametric build123d model, dimensioned drawings, Blender renders, BOM, analysis.
Pack: robion-maker-pack v{{PACK_VERSION}} — template change log & migration
steps live in MIGRATIONS.md in the pack repo.
This is a product-engineering project, not a software project — no tests or
linters; deliverables are drawings, renders, the BOM and the build sheet
(written in the language agreed with the user).
All dimensions are millimetres. Design decisions and the human-readable
BOM live in `README.md`, written in the language the deliverables use.

## Commands

```sh
uv sync                 # once; add --extra fea for analysis
make parts              # STEP+STL per part into out/parts/
make concept            # concept variants (parameter sets): previews + facts
make drawings           # dimensioned SVGs (+ PNG via the pattern rule)
make viz                # Blender renders (log-grepped for crashes)
make bom                # bill of materials into out/bom.md
make check              # assembly gate: mass/COG, interference, clearance
make cutlist            # cutting plan (out/cutlist.{md,svg}; -png variant)
make fea                # analysis (degrades to analytic-only without ccx)
make frame-fea          # beam-element frame model: stress plot + report
make buildsheet         # build sheet (out/build_sheet.html) + README.md + out/plan.md
make pdf                # one complete PDF: A4 build sheet + A3 drawing sheets
make doctor             # tool availability
```

Machine paths (this Mac): Chrome/OpenSCAD/KiCad as app bundles — see Makefile
variables; blender is on PATH.

## Architecture

- `model.py` — parameters (single source of truth) → derived values + asserts →
  `PARTS` registry (builder + material) → per-part exports + BOM. Never hardcode
  a dimension inside a builder.
- `drawings.py` — first-angle dimensioned views from `model.PARTS`.
- `cutlist.py` — STOCK declaration → FFD/shelf packing → cutting plan;
  stock/purchasing knowledge lives here, never in model.py.
- `blender_viz.py` — headless Cycles; per-part materials; bbox-driven scene.
- `fea.py` — analytic gate, then gmsh+pygccx+CalculiX.
- Verify visuals by rendering PNG and Reading it; Blender exit code is
  meaningless (Makefile greps the log).

## Design record

`design.json` in this folder is the design record: the idea, every answer and
who decided it, the chosen concept, the ten stages with their status and the
log of changes. It is written only by `design_record.py` next to it — never by
hand: `python3 design_record.py stage <id> working|needs_you|done`, `next <id>`,
`change <what> --from … --to … --invalidates …`, `check --strict` (rules: the
maker pack's `references/core/design-record.md`). Robion draws its design
panel from it, and a session that skips it leaves the panel lying.

## Cost of a change

Cheap (seconds — run freely while iterating): `make parts check bom`, the
live viewport. Expensive (minutes, order-dependent — run only after the
shape is frozen, and name which of them a change invalidates before
rerunning): `make viz`, `make drawings-png`, `make drawings-pdf`, `make fea`,
`make pdf`. A project with a PCB adds its own board chain here (placement
seconds, routing ~10 min, then fab outputs). Any parameter edit after the
concept freeze — including one forced by `make check` — is a change: record
it first with `python3 design_record.py change model.<param> --from … --to …
--invalidates …` (add `--fact key=value` when it moves a number on a concept
card), then regenerate.

